      "question": {
        ":question/id": "10d39822-0ceb-5bce-9342-8544fe8dcbe4",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "$\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{-7}{3 x + 1}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =3 x + 1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =3\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=3 x + 1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{-7}{3 x + 1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{- \\frac{7}{3}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{- \\frac{7}{3}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u & =- \\frac{7}{3}\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =- \\frac{7}{3}\\ln(u)|_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =- \\frac{7}{3}(\\ln(4)-\\ln(1)) \\\\  & =- \\frac{7}{3}\\ln((2)^{2}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{7}{3}\\cdot 2\\ln(2) \\\\  & =- \\frac{14 \\log{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
