        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "Evaluate the definite integral $\\int_{1}^{5}\\frac{3}{5 x + 2}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =5 x + 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =5 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{d}u & =3\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5 x + 2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 5 \\\\ u & 7 & 27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5}\\frac{3}{5 x + 2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{7}^{27}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{7}^{27}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{3}{5}\\int_{7}^{27}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\ln(u)|_{7}^{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(\\ln(27)-\\ln(7)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(\\ln((3)^{3})-\\ln(7)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(3\\ln(3)-\\ln(7)) \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 \\log{\\left(7 \\right)}}{5} + \\frac{9 \\log{\\left(3 \\right)}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
